# Van Share Cursor Rules

## Product Understanding

This project is a lightweight shared private transfer coordination platform.

It is NOT:
- Uber
- Grab
- a taxi dispatch app
- a realtime logistics platform

The goal is:
- helping travelers share private van transfers
- aggregating demand
- enabling operators to fulfill grouped journeys

---

# Technical Rules

## Keep architecture simple

Prefer:
- simple CRUD flows
- stable folder structure
- reusable components
- minimal dependencies

Avoid:
- unnecessary abstractions
- premature optimization
- large architectural rewrites

---

# UI Rules

## Mobile-first

Most users will be tourists on phones.

Prioritize:
- readability
- touch-friendly layouts
- fast loading
- clear CTAs

---

## High contrast only

Never use:
- pale gray text on white backgrounds
- low contrast buttons
- weak hover states

Explicitly define text colors where necessary.

---

## Reduce friction

Avoid unnecessary:
- signups
- account creation
- forms
- steps

Users should browse journeys immediately.

---

# Product Rules

## Preserve premium experience

Avoid recreating the downsides of shared vans.

Limit:
- excessive pickups
- long detours
- complicated routing

The product should still feel more premium than standard shared vans.

---

## Journey-first model

The core object is:
- journey

NOT:
- user accounts
- rides
- driver dispatch

---

# Mapping Rules

Maps are secondary UX.

Do not build:
- live tracking
- realtime driver maps
- complex route optimization

Simple pickup visualization is enough.

---

# Backend Rules

Use Supabase simply.

Prefer:
- straightforward queries
- predictable schema
- clear table naming

Use snake_case consistently.

Example:
- journeys
- journey_participants
- operator_claims

Avoid inconsistent naming.

---

# Folder Structure Rules

Do not repeatedly rename folders/components.

Maintain stable naming conventions.

Suggested structure:

/app
/components
/lib
/types

---

# Suggested App Structure

/app
  /(public)
    page.tsx
    journeys/page.tsx
    journeys/[id]/page.tsx
    create-journey/page.tsx
    join/[id]/page.tsx

  /operator
    dashboard/page.tsx
    journeys/page.tsx

  /api
    /journeys
    /participants
    /operators

/components
  /Journey
  /Operator
  /Map
  /UI

/lib
  /supabase
  /maps
  /utils

/types

---

# Database Philosophy

The database should reflect the business model.

Primary entities:
- journeys
- journey_participants
- operators
- operator_claims

Avoid overengineering user systems early.

Most users are tourists using the platform once.

---

# Journey Model

A journey represents:
- a planned shared private transfer
- usually created by one “host” traveler
- optionally joined by others

A journey should include:
- route
- date
- approximate departure time
- pickup area
- dropoff area
- passenger count
- luggage count
- host contact details
- status

---

# Participant Model

Participants join existing journeys.

Participants provide:
- name
- email
- WhatsApp/contact
- passenger count
- pickup/dropoff
- luggage count

No password/account required initially.

---

# Operator Model

Operators/drivers:
- browse grouped demand
- accept journeys manually
- coordinate externally if needed

Avoid dispatch complexity.

---

# Email Philosophy

Email is important for:
- notifications
- join requests
- confirmations
- operator coordination

Passwords are NOT important initially.

Prefer:
- magic links
- lightweight participation
- guest flows

---

# MVP Scope Control

Do NOT add early:
- payments
- chat systems
- live tracking
- realtime dispatch
- advanced auth
- automated route optimization
- pricing algorithms
- websocket systems

Focus on:
- browsing journeys
- creating journeys
- joining journeys
- operator coordination

---

# Map Philosophy

Maps should support the journey flow.

Maps are NOT the core product.

Simple use cases only:
- pickup visualization
- approximate route awareness
- basic geocoding

Avoid:
- realtime tracking
- turn-by-turn routing
- complex map interactions

---

# Engineering Philosophy

The hardest problem is:
- coordination friction

NOT:
- transportation technology

Keep the platform:
- lightweight
- understandable
- operationally realistic
- easy to maintain

---

# Code Quality Rules

Prefer:
- readable code
- explicit naming
- predictable structure
- small reusable components

Avoid:
- giant components
- deeply nested logic
- unnecessary abstractions
- inconsistent naming

---

# Styling Rules

Use:
- TailwindCSS
- explicit text colors
- consistent spacing
- large touch targets

Prefer:
- rounded cards
- simple layouts
- clear hierarchy

Avoid:
- clutter
- tiny text
- weak contrast
- overly “techy” dashboards

The app should feel:
- travel-oriented
- lightweight
- trustworthy
- approachable

---

# Final Product Reminder

This product is fundamentally:

> “Share a private van and pay less.”

Everything in the app should reinforce:
- simplicity
- coordination
- low friction
- premium shared travel